> this whole process of me trying to figure out the disconnect. Here is an 
> example of what I want to do, and how when I try to do the same 
> operation it fails:

If you mean the GRASS "Command console" tab in the GUI Layer Manager, it 
cannot be used to run interactive R. It can perhaps be used to run a 
single R script and exit. This is definitely not supported, and is 
illogical, as that tab is only for running modules that run and exit, 
writing to the standard output window above. I can start R in the Command 
console tab under Linux, but not under Windows XP.

By the way, using the full path to Rgui.exe from the MSYS prompt with & 
also works in Windows 7.

If you want to run R in GRASS on Windows, use Rgui.exe as I have 
explained, or possibly R.exe at the MSYS prompt. You have not shown why 
your proposed action has any sense or merit, and I think that asking 
people for their experienced doing something for which nothing was 
designed, and which cannot work, does show that you need to understand how 
computers and programs work. You could read the code of the Layer Manager 
Command console tab to see whether it could be improved to permit 
interactive programs to be run, but continuing this thread seems 
pointless. Asking unidentified users for their experiences when the person 
who wrote the R/GRASS interface has explained how to use it, is 
irritating, I'm afraid.

When the console reports that the command cannot be found, it is because 
it is not on the current search path, often the PATH environment variable, 
which is why one often uses an absolute address. On Windows XP, I cannot 
get any R executable to start from the Layer Manager Command console tab.

The "Command console" tab is not for running R interactively, period.

Roger


